@sidney-eliot Yeah, that's fair. But I didn't push the LODs to extremely low poly counts because it would look noticeably worse on Sketchfab. These are mostly meant as starting presets. If someone needs more aggressive optimization they can easily generate additional LODs themselves depending on their needs.

Same reason applies to why I didn't compress the textures manually. Different engines and pipelines use different compression formats anyway.

In short: I leave that up to you to decide how low the Polycount will be.

@C.W.R-official- I actually agree with you on that. Lately, I’ve been moving away from making strictly game-ready assets because it’s starting to feel like a losing battle. I’ve been putting much more effort into optimizing my models, only to have clients tell me afterward that I probably shouldn’t have spent that much time on optimization because modern hardware is powerful enough to handle it anyway.

What makes it even more complicated is that people are increasingly using AI-generated models. They can run them through something like Quad Remesher and get a perfectly usable background prop. The technology obviously still has limitations when it comes to producing genuinely high-quality assets, but it’s getting there. That makes it even harder to define what “game-ready” actually means nowadays.

You may have noticed that recently I’ve been uploading more models intended for rendering rather than games. They still have decent topology and are properly constructed, but I’m no longer trying to optimize every single polygon for a specific game budget. Like you said, the term “game-ready” has become pretty vague, and I think the industry is still figuring out what that actually means in the current workflow.

